WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.720
CHRIS LAW: Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.310
I'm Chris Law.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.890
And I'm a product manager
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:11.620
My mission is to make it easier
for our customers to do more

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.420
on Cloud with less effort.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.030
I'm excited to be here today
to tell you about Active Assist

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.920
and how it helps you do that.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.000
Look, Cloud can be complicated.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:20.570
You know it.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:21.160
We know it.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.125
Everyone knows it.

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:23.500
For many of you,
it can sometimes

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.240
feel like Cloud has
more knobs and dials

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.980
than this cockpit does.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.960
And this complexity
can be overwhelming.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.440
We've seen that almost
half of C-suite executives

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.250
said that complexity
was the factor that

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.375
would have the most negative
impacts on their Cloud

00:00:35.375 --> 00:00:37.940
computing ROI over
the next five years.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.940
In fact, in some
cases, complexity

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.190
can make the cost of
administration and maintenance

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.258
higher than the costs of
software and hardware.

00:00:45.258 --> 00:00:47.050
When we dig into the
effects of complexity,

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.950
we see a few big buckets.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.280
First, complexity
can cause waste.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:52.830
Things like VMs are
over-provisioned

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.580
and discounts aren't
optimized, all

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.990
leading to unnecessary costs.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:59.500
Second, complexity can
cause security gaps.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.810
We see things like access
permissions being given away

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.060
too freely and firewall
rule configurations

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.530
that are so
confusing that people

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.270
are afraid to change them.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.840
Finally, all of this ends up
causing lots of extra work.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.050
Things get misconfigured.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.592
And now you've got to spend your
team's time trying to figure

00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:16.115
out how to troubleshoot it all.

00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:17.740
Now, what makes this
worse is that some

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.448
of the traditional
approaches that people

00:01:19.448 --> 00:01:22.750
take to solve these challenges
don't work well or don't scale.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.820
Some companies try to
keep hiring more people

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:25.872
to address the problem.

00:01:25.872 --> 00:01:28.330
But the reality is that the
talent pool available is small.

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.140
And it takes forever to hire.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.470
We at Google face
this issue too.

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.200
Other companies go ahead
and just buy more resources.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.910
But that ends up
being unsustainable

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.010
and adds more complexity.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.960
And finally, some
others have tried just

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.770
to write more code
or widgets to help.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.770
But we hear from them
that maintaining that code

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:48.250
is costly, and ultimately,
it's not their core mission.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.460
At Google, we believe the answer
is intelligence and automation.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.370
Today's IT environments,
whether on-prem or in the Cloud,

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.740
feel like a car from the 1970s.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:58.910
Everything is manual.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.660
And you've got to look
over your shoulder,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.500
shift manually,
look at a paper map,

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:04.520
all while getting
bad gas mileage.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:06.950
We want to upgrade your
Cloud and provide experiences

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.600
like modern cars and beyond.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.729
How can we provide tools
like blind spot sensors

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:13.700
and GPS that make your current
Cloud experience markedly

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.870
better?

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.947
Ultimately, we're on
a journey to a future

00:02:16.947 --> 00:02:19.280
where we want to be able to
automate everything for you.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.578
It won't happen overnight.

00:02:20.578 --> 00:02:23.120
But we are looking to move your
Cloud forward in the same way

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.990
that Google's Waymo is
moving cars forward.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.330
We're excited to introduce
you to Active Assist,

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:30.460
here to actively assist you.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:31.943
So what is Active Assist?

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:33.610
It's a new solution
that brings together

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.910
an array of intelligent features
which all have a common goal--

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.310
to make it easier for you
to optimize your Cloud

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.060
while reducing manual effort.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.910
The best of both worlds, right?

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.560
We asked ourselves,
what could we

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.240
do to empower
customers with better

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.980
intelligence and automation?

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.480
And after speaking
with many of you,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.880
we heard three answers
loud and clear.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.550
First, you want to find out
what went wrong quickly so you

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.290
can take action right away.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:57.790
Second, you want
to prevent mistakes

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.440
from happening altogether.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.920
And third, you want to
improve your Cloud easily.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.260
By doing this, we can
deliver on three big things

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.710
for our customers.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.440
First, we're going
to give you more ways

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.510
to understand how to
optimize your resources

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:12.100
and let you automate that.

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.020
Second, we're going to
help you proactively

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:15.460
secure your Cloud
by alerting you

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.710
to things that need fixing
before they become a bigger

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:18.730
issue.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.550
And third, all of this aims
to make your lives easier

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:23.800
by reducing the time and
toil that troubleshooting,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.170
analyzing, configuring, and
administering your Cloud

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.520
usually takes.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.140
At the end of the
day, Active Assist

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.140
aims to help you maximize your
Cloud's overall performance

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.350
without you having to be on the
hook to understand every nook

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.180
and cranny of your Cloud.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.472
Your Cloud should work for you.

00:03:38.472 --> 00:03:40.847
And that's what we aim to do
by helping you automatically

00:03:40.847 --> 00:03:42.190
optimize your resources.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.140
For example, we've had
customers like Viewclip

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.432
who have been able to take
advantage of recommendations

00:03:46.432 --> 00:03:49.130
to quickly optimize
their VMs and save money.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:50.710
It's not enough to
save money though.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.350
Today security is also
top of mind for everyone.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:54.940
We're in an age
where organizations

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.220
have more and more sensitive
customer information

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.930
and hacks can be devastating.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:01.535
No organization wants to be
on the receiving end of that.

00:04:01.535 --> 00:04:03.160
With Active Assist,
we're going to help

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.590
you proactively identify
security gaps and maintain

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:06.980
best practices.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.467
In fact, we've had
large customers

00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:10.800
who were able to use identity
and access management role

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.170
recommendations to help make
their environments more secure,

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:17.829
while reducing the number of
permission that were granted.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.899
Active Assist will help you by
using automation intelligence

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:22.842
to bring key actions
right to your fingertips.

00:04:22.842 --> 00:04:25.300
It takes away the guesswork
and the mystery that's normally

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.250
required to operate your Cloud.

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.900
For example, customers
like HIPAA Vault

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.442
were able to use
Network Intelligence

00:04:30.442 --> 00:04:33.070
Center to quickly troubleshoot
networking configuration

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:34.540
issues.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.970
By now, you may be
asking, OK, but how

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.710
is Active Assist
going to do all this?

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.210
The fun answer is
that Active Assist

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:43.180
is going to unlock Google
magic across your Cloud.

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.890
We're going to use
our specialities

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.080
around data analytics
and machine learning

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.670
to power Active Assist.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.140
Active Assist is
a set of features

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.820
that cuts across Google Cloud.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.970
So whether you're interested
in security, cost, networking,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.910
compute, data, or our
newest offering, Anthos,

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.550
we'll be rolling out a
number of features to help.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.530
To show you some practical
examples of this,

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:05.488
I'm going to turn it over
to Dima Melnyk, who's

00:05:05.488 --> 00:05:07.635
a product manager on our team.

00:05:07.635 --> 00:05:08.760
DIMA MELNYK: Thanks, Chris.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:09.900
Hi, everyone.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:12.690
I'm excited to show you how some
of these intelligent features

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.490
that live under the
Active Assist umbrella

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.310
can help you solve some of the
practical day-to-day challenges

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:18.480
you face.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.210
And to do this, I'm going
to tell you a little story.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:22.990
This is Steve.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:26.273
And he is in charge
of an application.

00:05:26.273 --> 00:05:28.440
He helped to architect and
set up the infrastructure

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.670
for this application.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:32.370
And it's been smooth
sailing so far.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.780
This said, he built
it for normal times.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:36.990
But now with people
generally at home more,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.240
buying patterns changed
almost overnight,

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.550
skyrocketing the traffic
to their application.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.670
And scale is hard.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.680
Steve and his
colleagues face a number

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.300
of challenges with
their application's

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.760
performance, cost, and security.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.510
First thing in the
morning, he gets a call

00:05:54.510 --> 00:05:56.760
from Alice, who is in charge
of their customer support

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:57.750
department.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.660
Alice tells Steve they got
slammed with customer calls

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.970
over the weekend, complaining
about terrible log

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.648
times and overall slowness.

00:06:05.648 --> 00:06:07.690
Steve starts to think
about what could be causing

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.310
those performance issues.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:10.930
He contemplates a
couple of places

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:13.100
where they could be hiding.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:17.610
Is it compute capacity, or
perhaps networking, or the data

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.470
infrastructure
layer that he knows

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.445
was architected
with some duct tape?

00:06:21.445 --> 00:06:23.820
Or maybe it's not even caused
by the Cloud infrastructure

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.570
he's in charge of at all.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:27.870
Honestly, it could be anything.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.310
And the clock is ticking.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:30.940
And Steve needs to move fast.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:33.030
He's worried that going
down the checklist

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:35.090
might take way too
much time and effort,

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:36.900
while his users
continue to experience

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.330
slowness and downtime.

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:42.550
Steve is not really exactly
sure about what to do next.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.280
But then he remembers
a new solution

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:47.440
that might be able to
help, Active Assist.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.090
He recalls that
Active Assist's goal

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.950
is to help folks
like him optimize

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:53.680
their Cloud with recommendations
and other tools which

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.190
reduce administrative overhead.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:57.600
This sounds really promising.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.430
So he pulls up the
Recommendation Hub

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.970
in the Google Cloud Console.

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:03.400
Steve is pretty excited.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.530
Right away, he is seeing
some recommendations

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.430
on how to optimize resources
in his project for performance

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.978
and high availability.

00:07:10.978 --> 00:07:13.270
He had a feeling that computer
resources on his project

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.560
might be overloaded with
all this new demand.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.100
So he navigates to the VM
rightsizing recommendations

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.260
first.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:23.230
He can immediately see that
many VMs on this project

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:26.410
have recently had extremely
high CPU utilization, which

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:28.315
can definitely
result in long queues

00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:30.190
and negatively impact
the overall performance

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:31.990
of his application.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.990
And this all is based on
Google Cloud performing

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.990
multiple simulations to
select the replacement VMs

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.550
optimal for Steve's workload.

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.890
So he is confident to apply
these recommendations.

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:42.750
It almost feels too
good to be true.

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.850
Things like this used
to take a lot more time

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.260
to investigate and fix.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.497
And yet, this can now be
done with only a few clicks.

00:07:50.497 --> 00:07:52.330
Now Steve is thinking
about how to best deal

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.430
with the spikes in
demand, just like the one

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:57.080
they saw over the
weekend, going forward.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:07:59.020
So he turns to the next
intelligent feature

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:00.940
in Active Assist's arsenal.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:04.088
Steve already relies on Compute
Engine's real-time Autoscaler.

00:08:04.088 --> 00:08:06.130
But it appears, though,
that it doesn't work well

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.230
for his monolithic
Java application, which

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:11.160
takes more than five
minutes to initialize.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:12.800
This autoscaling
latency contributes

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.842
to the availability issues
that some of his users

00:08:14.842 --> 00:08:16.330
have reported.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.580
So he enables
Predictive Autoscaler,

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.550
which forecasts future capacity
needs based on historical load

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:25.850
data, and starts up VM instances
ahead of upcoming demand.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:26.510
Awesome.

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:30.960
Steve just took another
manual task off his plate.

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.570
Next, he looks into the
other Auto-healing feature.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:35.640
As his team's frequent
on-call person,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.409
Steve is definitely
not looking to get

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:40.539
paged in the middle of the night
because of an application crash

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:43.350
caused by a recent bad update,
memory overflow, or something

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:44.370
else.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.080
To prevent this
from happening, he

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.150
creates a simple health
check and enables

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.070
Compute Engine's Auto-healing.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.380
This feature will
automatically recreate

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.920
unhealthy VMs to reduce
the risk of any downtime.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.010
And the best thing, it works
for his stateful workload,

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:02.238
preserving his
applications data.

00:09:02.238 --> 00:09:04.780
Now, that he has addressed his
applications' compute capacity

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.310
issues that have
been contributing

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:08.200
to the poor performance,
Steve decides

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.740
to go deal with that original
issue that Alice has reported,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.540
where some of their
users in Europe

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.300
were impacted more than
the rest of the world.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.260
He suspects these problems
might be caused by the network.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:20.847
And he isn't the
networking expert.

00:09:20.847 --> 00:09:22.930
And Steve would typically
hand something like this

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.700
off to the networking
team for investigation.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.670
But as he's fighting
the clock, he

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.980
decides to open the
network intelligence center

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.890
and see what he can
find out on his own.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:34.840
So he looks at the
performance dashboard

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:37.750
and quickly confirms that
everything within GCP

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.250
looks good.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.410
So he opens up the
network topology view

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.300
to investigate if
there are any problems

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:45.520
with the external access
that might be contributing

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:47.230
to the high latency.

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:49.270
From this view,
he can immediately

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:50.810
see that something is wrong.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:52.810
Their users in Europe
are, for some reason,

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:56.902
being served from the
nonoptimal US Central 1 region.

00:09:56.902 --> 00:09:59.360
He gives a quick call to his
friend on the networking team.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.018
And they quickly
realize this must

00:10:01.018 --> 00:10:02.810
have been caused by
the oversubscribed pool

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.480
of compute resources, which
resulted in the global log

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.220
balancer automatically
rerouting traffic to the US.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:12.030
They remedied this issue
quickly by adding more resources

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.215
in the Europe region and
reconfiguring the load

00:10:14.215 --> 00:10:14.715
balancer.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.530
And while on this topic of
the networking optimization

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:22.620
opportunities, Steve also
notices this VPN tunnel

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.600
over utilization recommendation.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.130
This wasn't even top of mind.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:30.000
But fixing this now could
prevent major headaches later.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.790
Looks like one of the VPN
tunnels on his project

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.100
has been approaching
its utilization capacity

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.840
and is likely to turn
into a bottleneck

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.730
within the next few days.

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.100
So he proactively notifies
the networking team

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:43.590
to make sure they look
into adding some redundancy

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:48.815
before this over-utilized
VPN becomes a real problem.

00:10:48.815 --> 00:10:51.980
Now, Steve also had a hunch
that his data infrastructure

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:54.690
might not have been
optimally set up either.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.660
Active Assist helps him
to identify one more

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.460
immediate opportunity
to optimize

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:00.860
his system for resiliency.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:02.780
It turns out that
one of his teams

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.780
has been using Bigtable
without backing up data.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.550
If one of Steve's
developer colleagues

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.800
makes a mistake in
their business logic

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:12.320
and writes some gibberish
into a critical table,

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.920
it can be very difficult
to recover from that state.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.560
Following this recommendation,
with just one click,

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.590
Steve creates a Dataflow
job to continuously backup

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.780
Bigtable data into
Cloud Storage to make

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.780
sure it can be restored in
the event of a system failure,

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:30.270
or let's face it, user error.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.450
This seems like another
major improvement

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:35.773
that will let him sleep
through the night, page free.

00:11:35.773 --> 00:11:37.190
So Steve calls
Alice and tells her

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:38.690
about a number of
important changes

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:40.880
to the applications'
back end he implemented

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:43.280
to remediate all the
performance issues reported

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.290
by their customers.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.420
They both are relieved
that all these changes were

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:50.420
made in just a couple of hours
and with basically no labors,

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:53.060
investigation, custom
scripts, or any significant

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:55.510
administrative overhead.

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:57.760
Excited, Steve goes
back to his desk

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.010
and continues poking
around other optimization

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:01.360
opportunities.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:03.730
He discovers that, in addition
to applying Active Assist

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:06.790
recommendations from within
the Google Cloud Console,

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:09.400
he could also query the
recommender API directly

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:12.220
and incorporate them as a part
of his organization's existing

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.250
workflow.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.200
So he decides to
go the extra mile.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.630
And he writes a
code custom script

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:21.240
that pulls new recommendations
from the recommender API

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:24.690
and delivers a digest to
his inbox on a weekly basis.

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.880
Steve also shares this
script with his colleagues,

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:29.850
who run other projects, to
help them proactively address

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:31.580
some of these performance
issues as well.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:38.270
But wait, as soon as he is
done helping Alice manage

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.870
this initial performance
application performance crisis,

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:44.490
he has got Bob, their finance
director, knocking at his door.

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.550
Bob needs for everyone
across the company

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:49.790
to cut their costs
by at least 25%.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.130
Steve never really liked Bob.

00:12:52.130 --> 00:12:54.510
But they sat down together
to think through a few cost

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.750
optimization opportunities.

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:00.050
First, there's the total cost
of ownership perspective.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:01.580
Is it time to start
re-architecting

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.550
their self-managed stack and
go completely serverless?

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:08.388
Or maybe they should go use
a different database service.

00:13:08.388 --> 00:13:10.180
Or there could be ways
for them to optimize

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:11.740
their current resources.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.800
But how can they do this without
sacrificing their application's

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.800
performance and availability?

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.480
And finally, there's
the pure finance angle.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.340
With all the cloud products
Steve's team is relying on,

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.340
there is a lot of complexity
on the billing side

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:28.790
alone, with literally
hundreds of billable features,

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:32.690
a number of pricing models
and discounts to pick from.

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:34.670
Just like troubleshooting
performance issues,

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:37.040
cost-cutting can be really
hard and can take up

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:39.600
a lot of time and effort.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.540
Short of gathering a team of
architects, DevOps, and finance

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.820
folks, Steve decides
to go take a look

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:48.150
at what Active Assist has
to offer in this space.

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:50.040
So he pulls up
Recommendation Hub

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.620
and focuses on cost
optimization cards this time.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:55.790
Right away, he is seeing
some recommendations

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:58.850
for his applications,
computer resources, storage,

00:13:58.850 --> 00:13:59.985
and billing.

00:13:59.985 --> 00:14:02.610
And he is already familiar with
the rightsizing recommendations

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.090
for performance.

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.360
So he starts with the ones
that look very similar

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:11.030
but appear to be geared
towards cost savings.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.850
Steve realizes there is a
number of VMs on his project

00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:15.740
that are way too
large for the task

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.550
that they have been
initially allocated for.

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:20.100
He applies these
recommendations,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.300
realizing substantial
savings from removing

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:25.390
this extra compute capacity
his project didn't really need.

00:14:27.845 --> 00:14:29.470
After addressing the
over-provisioning,

00:14:29.470 --> 00:14:32.230
he turns to the
unused resources.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.510
This turns out to be
another major contributor

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:37.250
to the high Cloud
spend on his project.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:39.080
These recommendations
indicate that he

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:41.150
has got a lot of idle
VMs on his project,

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:44.420
possibly from previous
testing or development cycles.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:46.250
He didn't expect
to see this many.

00:14:46.250 --> 00:14:48.060
But he's not really
surprised, as he

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.730
knows how easy it is for
people to spin up a VM

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.587
and forget about it.

00:14:53.587 --> 00:14:55.170
And while on this
topic, he also comes

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:57.690
across another type of vital
resource recommendation.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.660
Turns out, there are quite a few
persistent disks on his project

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:03.060
that haven't been
utilized either.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:05.610
He realizes substantial
savings by deleting

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.400
most of those idle PDs
and archiving the rest

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:10.315
into cold storage.

00:15:10.315 --> 00:15:11.690
And Steve is glad
that they won't

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:14.420
be paying for all these
unused resources going forward

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.670
and proceeds to look at the
billing model optimization

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:20.220
opportunities to
further reduce costs.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.920
He looked into this Committed
Use Discount concept

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.310
a few months ago.

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:27.030
But it seemed just way too
hard to operationalize.

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:30.240
Before Active Assist, he had to
do a lot of things on his own,

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.670
starting from understanding
their team's current usage

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:34.800
patterns to figuring
out trade-offs,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:37.830
versus their current billing
model, which leverages

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:39.760
sustained use
discounts, and finally,

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.450
to projecting the impact of
any potential billing model

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:43.800
changes.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.740
So he is very glad to see that
Active Assist has already done

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.030
all this heavy lifting for him.

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:51.760
He grabs the screenshot
with projected savings

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:54.100
and sends an email to
Bob, the finance director,

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:57.010
to get his approval to switch
from the on-demand pricing

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:58.630
to a three-year
commitment, which

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:02.290
could slash their compute
cost by almost 50%.

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:04.030
As he moves to the
other billing model

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:05.680
optimization
opportunities, he sees

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:08.050
that Active Assist has
more recommendations

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:09.430
for his project.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:13.210
They have been using
BigQuery as a data warehouse.

00:16:13.210 --> 00:16:15.340
As their application
data has been growing,

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:17.730
more and more people
have been using BigQuery

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.720
With their current
on-demand billing model,

00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:23.540
their cost has been
steadily going up as well.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.540
This recommendation
is for Steve's project

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:28.480
to consider switching to the
BigQuery slot billing model,

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:30.130
where they can run
as many queries

00:16:30.130 --> 00:16:33.220
as they need on a fixed
amount of [INAUDIBLE] slots.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:35.600
Based on his team's
usage history,

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.590
Active Assist estimated they
will save a lot on their bill

00:16:38.590 --> 00:16:41.080
without negatively
impacting performance, which

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:44.720
is really important to Steve.

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:47.780
So he takes this up with Bob,
along with his earlier proposal

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.717
to switch to the Committed Use
Discounts for compute engine.

00:16:50.717 --> 00:16:52.550
They walk through the
resource optimizations

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:54.150
that Steve has
already implemented,

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:56.300
which resulted in
immediate savings,

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.190
and also agree on
implementing the billing

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.680
model changes that are
expected to yield even more

00:17:00.680 --> 00:17:03.960
substantial savings over time.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.770
And as they wrap
up, Bob asks Steve

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:09.650
if he can help apply similar
techniques to other projects

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:12.310
in their organization.

00:17:12.310 --> 00:17:14.859
Steve goes back to his
desk to read the manual

00:17:14.859 --> 00:17:17.800
and quickly discovers there is
BigQuery Export feature, which

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.800
allows him to dump all these
insights and recommendations

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.790
to a BigQuery
table and do custom

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:25.780
analysis across their
entire organization.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.515
Steve is already
familiar with BigQuery

00:17:27.515 --> 00:17:29.140
since they have been
using it for a lot

00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:31.925
of their operational
data anyway.

00:17:31.925 --> 00:17:34.300
So a few hours later, he sends
Bob, the finance director,

00:17:34.300 --> 00:17:37.990
a link to the dashboard in
Google Cloud's Data Studio

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:39.610
that uncovers
substantial cost savings

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:43.420
opportunities across multiple
projects in their organization.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:45.520
Having optimized their
cloud infrastructure

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.600
for cost and performance,
Steve turns to security.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:52.390
His organization never
took security lightly.

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:54.910
And Steve feels they have a
good set of in-depth security

00:17:54.910 --> 00:17:57.260
measures in place.

00:17:57.260 --> 00:17:59.280
Provided their recent
growth, though, as well as

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:01.393
their company's new
work-from-home configuration,

00:18:01.393 --> 00:18:03.810
he meets up with Molly, who
is in charge of their security

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.430
team.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:07.710
Together, they sit down
to reassess their security

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:09.720
measures and make sure
they are doing everything

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:11.760
they can to protect the
sensitive information

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:13.593
that their customers
are trusting them with.

00:18:15.950 --> 00:18:19.590
A couple of things come to
mind as they go down the list.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.240
Do people have the permissions
they need to do their jobs,

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:23.675
but not more?

00:18:23.675 --> 00:18:25.050
Over-granted
permissions can lead

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:27.330
to incidents,
malicious in nature,

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.850
as well as the ones caused by
simple, unintentional human

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:33.540
errors, which can also result
in data loss or infrastructure

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.740
changes that can be
really hard to revert.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:38.450
Then there's the
networking layer.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.820
Molly tells Steve she's
worried about this one,

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:43.420
especially after reading a
breach about a competitor who

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:45.560
had an open port,
which permitted traffic

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:47.420
from an insecure
zone, which then

00:18:47.420 --> 00:18:50.480
went through a
misconfigured firewall.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:54.152
Are there any holes in their
firewall configuration?

00:18:54.152 --> 00:18:56.110
And there's a whole set
of other considerations

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:59.330
around protecting sensitive data
as well as their very own power

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:01.180
users, who present
a high security

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:04.330
risk if their identities
are compromised.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.140
Steve knows these issues
can be really hard.

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:09.170
But he feels optimistic,
since he already

00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:13.290
knows that Active Assist
should be able to help.

00:19:13.290 --> 00:19:15.870
This time around, he looks at
the security-related features.

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.287
And he is excited to see some
identity and access, as well

00:19:18.287 --> 00:19:21.720
as firewall-related
recommendations.

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:25.430
He decides to dig into
the permissions first.

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.500
Steve, like many project
owners out there,

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:29.720
wants to enforce the
principle of list privilege,

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:31.850
when all the project
members have access only

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:33.780
to what they really need.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:36.980
He knows, though, that solving
this at scale is really hard.

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:39.680
Many things can go wrong, from
breaking something running

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.900
in production to
slowing his teams down

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:43.670
with way too much process.

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:46.185
It is much easier
said than done.

00:19:46.185 --> 00:19:47.560
That's why he's
extremely excited

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:49.540
to see Active Assist's
identity and access

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:51.670
management rightsizing
recommendations that

00:19:51.670 --> 00:19:54.220
go beyond just removing
unused permissions,

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:57.040
by using machine learning
algorithms to figure out what

00:19:57.040 --> 00:19:59.510
permissions users really need.

00:19:59.510 --> 00:20:02.540
So he quickly cleans up all
the inactive service accounts

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:05.660
and also grants smaller and
granular roles to his project

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.270
members, literally making
hundreds of accounts

00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:11.010
more secure with
only a few clicks.

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:12.510
And as he goes
through this process,

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:14.280
he realizes that he
would love to dig

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:15.960
into a couple of
additional questions

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.130
in the context of
some of these changes.

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.580
For example, how can I see
who can act as the service

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:22.570
account on my project?

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:26.590
Luckily, Active Assist has him
covered in this one as well.

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:30.010
With Policy Analyzer, Steve is
able to answer this question

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:31.060
and many more.

00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:32.830
In addition to the
one he had in mind,

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:34.600
he runs a couple
of other queries.

00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:36.560
Which VMs can John delete?

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:39.040
Who has right access on
this BigQuery data set?

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:40.970
It feels like magic.

00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:42.070
But wait a minute.

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:44.840
Steve sees a service account
that is able to access

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:48.870
the BigQuery data set that
it probably shouldn't.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.862
He wants to remove the
service account access,

00:20:50.862 --> 00:20:53.070
but wants to make sure that
he doesn't break anything

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.680
in the process.

00:20:54.680 --> 00:20:57.320
With Policy Simulator, he
can check the proposed policy

00:20:57.320 --> 00:21:00.140
changes against the
90-day access history

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.390
to see if these changes would
block any essential access

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:05.711
which was previously used.

00:21:05.711 --> 00:21:08.560
And just as Steve wraps
up with Policy Simulator,

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:11.070
he has someone come to
him with this dreaded,

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:13.950
"I'm getting a denied
error" problem.

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:16.380
These seemingly simple
issues can be surprisingly

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:17.480
hard to troubleshoot.

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:19.230
They can be caused by
a number of factors.

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.690
It could be a lack of a
certain permission, or group

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:23.820
membership, or a
combination of IM

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:27.300
and network policies
causing this denial.

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:29.450
So Steve is relieved to
be able to use the Policy

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:33.120
Troubleshooter to confirm that
this particular user never

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:36.040
had any access granted to
them in the first place.

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:37.830
So he adds them
to a group, which

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:39.720
provides just the
right amount of access

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:42.700
they need to do their job.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:44.740
Steve feels that he
is now in the position

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:47.230
to not only establish the
principle of list privilege,

00:21:47.230 --> 00:21:48.700
but actually
maintain it, instead

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:50.980
of simply granting all
permissions to everyone

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:53.182
who comes to him with
"my access isn't working"

00:21:53.182 --> 00:21:53.890
type of an issue.

00:21:57.070 --> 00:21:58.690
Next, he turns to the network.

00:21:58.690 --> 00:22:01.870
Firewall is one of the most
fundamental protection layers.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:05.200
Managing, however, is a
major challenge for Steve.

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:07.390
His firewall configuration
grew really complex

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:10.220
as more and more access
was added over time.

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:12.160
So he is afraid
to even touch it,

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.327
as he knows that this will
likely break something.

00:22:15.327 --> 00:22:17.410
That's why Steve is relieved
to see these firewall

00:22:17.410 --> 00:22:19.060
insights from Active Assist.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:21.310
He starts with the
unused firewall rules,

00:22:21.310 --> 00:22:23.920
which can potentially
allow an unwanted access

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.680
and present a major security
risk to his organization.

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:30.180
Steve then looks at the
firewall optimization insights.

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.770
Active Assist also brings
to his attention things

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:35.350
like unreachable and
misconfigured roles,

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:36.820
which could be redundant.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:38.710
He's happy to see
these insights as well,

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.380
as they help him simplify
his firewall configuration

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:43.060
and make it much
easier to manage

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:46.450
and maintain going forward.

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:48.950
But as he cleans up his
firewall configuration,

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:51.285
this thought crosses
Steve's mind again.

00:22:51.285 --> 00:22:52.910
What if I break
something in production

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:54.860
by doing these changes?

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:59.080
Luckily, Active Assist has him
covered in this one as well.

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:00.580
Using the Connectivity
Test feature

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:02.350
from the Network
Intelligence Center,

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:05.350
Steve can double check the
impact of configuration changes

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:07.020
as he applies them.

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:09.670
And this gives him confidence
in a couple of especially tricky

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:12.280
scenarios, where if
he wasn't 100% sure

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.470
if it was OK to remove a
particular firewall rule.

00:23:17.780 --> 00:23:20.210
Having his firewall
configuration optimized,

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.850
Steve turns to one
more security gap.

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:25.730
He heard at a recent
conference that roughly 30%

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:29.490
of annual enterprise data
breaches involve phishing.

00:23:29.490 --> 00:23:31.360
He knows he should
look into this.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.090
But it's tough to
roll out and enforce

00:23:33.090 --> 00:23:36.870
a policy around authentication
with multiple teams, systems,

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:39.920
and vendors that his
company is relying on.

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:44.380
The good news is he can now
turn to Active Assist for help.

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:47.340
He enables a phone as a
security key recommendation

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:50.460
for his organization's
most at-risk users,

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:52.590
like the ones who
have broad permissions

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:54.990
and a substantial blast
radius if their accounts

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:56.980
are compromised.

00:23:56.980 --> 00:23:59.440
And the best thing,
it's free and only

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.670
takes two minutes to set up.

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:05.050
Happy about the
substantial progress

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:08.140
he was able to make so quickly,
Steve sits down with Molly

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.700
to go over these
important security

00:24:09.700 --> 00:24:11.590
gaps he was able to close.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:13.455
And it's a win-win for everyone.

00:24:13.455 --> 00:24:15.580
The project team can still
maintain the flexibility

00:24:15.580 --> 00:24:17.980
and velocity, thanks to the
efficient troubleshooting

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:21.070
and analysis tooling that Active
Assist provides in addition

00:24:21.070 --> 00:24:24.340
to the security insights and
recommendations themselves.

00:24:24.340 --> 00:24:27.100
And the security team is happy
with the substantially reduced

00:24:27.100 --> 00:24:29.110
attack surface,
which is getting them

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:33.500
much closer to the zero-trust
environment that they want.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:37.340
Molly asks Steve if he can
help make this work at scale

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:39.380
with other projects
in their organization,

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:41.427
and especially with
their new GitOps approach

00:24:41.427 --> 00:24:43.760
to administering the most
sensitive parts of their cloud

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.070
infrastructure,
such as permissions.

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:47.960
They want to use
GitHub to do everything

00:24:47.960 --> 00:24:50.910
declaratively and centrally.

00:24:50.910 --> 00:24:53.780
And Steve already
has the answer.

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:57.930
While looking into the
recommender API and BigQuery

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:01.010
Export earlier, he came
across this reference solution

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:04.340
in the documentation, which can
help them do exactly what Molly

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:06.070
is asking for.

00:25:06.070 --> 00:25:08.650
He downloads this reference
implementation from GitHub,

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:09.610
polishes it.

00:25:09.610 --> 00:25:12.490
And a couple of hours later,
folks on their security team

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:14.650
start seeing some
recommendations presented

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:18.340
as pull requests against their
configuration stored in Git.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:24.520
To recap, we looked
at several examples

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:26.620
